## Goal
Do the six samples separate by treatment? Run a principal component analysis on the gene-level counts from the rnaseq_bulk assay and plot the samples in PC space, coloured treated versus untreated.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| `counts_gene` | 02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper (native) | `02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv` |
| `design` | 01_prepare_samplesheets (native) | `01_samplesheets/rnaseq_bulk_design.csv` |

## Method
Environment: `gars-bio` (pandas, scikit-learn, matplotlib). Everything runs as one script, `scripts/sample_pca.py`, submitted through the executor. Inputs are read-only.

1. Read the count matrix (columns `gene_id`, `gene_name`, then one column per sample: A1, A2, A3, B1, B2, B3) and the design table (`sample_id`, `condition`).
2. Check that the sample columns and the design's `sample_id` values match exactly; stop with an error otherwise.
3. Drop genes with a total count below 10 across all samples.
4. Transform: log2(count + 1). No further scaling or normalisation (the counts are already length-scaled by the pipeline).
5. Centre each gene to mean zero, no unit-variance scaling. PCA with `sklearn.decomposition.PCA` (all components, `svd_solver="full"`) on the samples x genes matrix.
6. Write the per-sample PC coordinates and the variance explained per PC.
7. Plot PC1 vs PC2, one point per sample, coloured by `condition` (treated / untreated), points labelled with sample ID, axes labelled with the percent variance explained.

